      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a large heavy frying pan or casserole over medium-high heat. Brown the {{lamb-shoulder}} of lamb pieces in batches, 2 to 3 minutes per side. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lower the heat to medium. Add the onion and cook for 12 minutes until golden and beginning to stick to the pan."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic, ginger, garam masala, coriander, cumin, chilli powder, and turmeric. Cook for 3 minutes, stirring constantly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add {{tinned-tomatoes}} of tinned tomatoes and stir well. Return the lamb to the pan. Add 200 ml of water. Cover and cook over low heat for 1 to 1.5 hours until the lamb is tender."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the lid. Raise the heat to medium and cook uncovered, stirring frequently, until the sauce has reduced to a thick, sticky coating on the lamb. This is the bhuna stage and takes 15 to 20 minutes. Stir constantly in the final 5 minutes to prevent the sauce catching."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season with salt, scatter with fresh coriander, and serve with rice or bread."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "symptom": "Sauce burns at the bottom during reduction",
              "cause": "Heat too high or not stirring frequently enough",
              "fix": "Lower the heat to medium and stir every 30 seconds during the reduction stage. A heavy-based pan is less likely to catch."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Sauce is still liquid after 20 minutes of reduction",
              "cause": "Tomatoes added too much liquid or lamb released a lot of moisture",
              "fix": "Continue reducing at medium-high heat. A bhuna takes longer than most restaurant versions suggest. The sauce should pull away from the sides of the pan."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bhuna is less commonly ordered than tikka masala or korma. It has a dedicated following among people who prefer a drier, more intense curry. The technique comes from Bengali cooking, where frying aromatics for a long time is a core skill. British curry houses sell bhuna as a style: bhuna chicken, bhuna lamb, and bhuna prawn all share the same thick, reduced sauce."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
